Roy Fielding's dissertation defined six constraints for REST: **client-server**, **stateless**, **cacheable**, **uniform interface**, **layered system**, and **code-on-demand** (optional). Most "REST APIs" satisfy only two or three. Here is an endpoint that satisfies them all.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping(value = "/orders", produces = MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
public class OrderController {

    private final OrderService service;
    private final OrderMapper mapper;

    // ---- Uniform interface: resource URL + standard HTTP verbs + HATEOAS links ----
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<EntityModel<OrderDto>> get(@PathVariable Long id,
                                                      WebRequest request) {
        Order order = service.find(id);

        // ---- Cacheable: ETag + conditional GET ----
        String etag = """ + order.getVersion() + """;
        if (request.checkNotModified(etag)) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_MODIFIED).eTag(etag).build();
        }

        EntityModel<OrderDto> model = EntityModel.of(mapper.toDto(order),
            linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).get(id, null)).withSelfRel(),
            linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).cancel(id)).withRel("cancel"));

        return ResponseEntity.ok()
            .eTag(etag)
            .cacheControl(CacheControl.maxAge(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS).cachePublic())
            .body(model);
    }

    // ---- Stateless: every request carries its own auth; no session ----
    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderDto> create(@Valid @RequestBody OrderRequest req,
                                           @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String key,
                                           @AuthenticationPrincipal Jwt jwt) {
        Order order = service.createIdempotent(req, key, jwt.getSubject());
        URI location = linkTo(methodOn(OrderController.class).get(order.getId(), null)).toUri();
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(mapper.toDto(order));
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> cancel(@PathVariable Long id) {
        service.cancel(id);
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

```http
# A fully-formed request/response conversation
GET /orders/42 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...           ← stateless: request carries identity
Accept: application/json
If-None-Match: "7"                            ← cacheable: conditional GET

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
ETag: "7"
Cache-Control: public, max-age=30
{
  "id": 42, "status": "PAID", "total": 99.90,
  "_links": {                                 ← uniform interface: HATEOAS
    "self":   { "href": "/orders/42" },
    "cancel": { "href": "/orders/42" }
  }
}
```

- **Client-server**: UI and API evolve independently; the server exposes resources, not UI widgets.
- **Stateless**: `Authorization` header on every request; no server session. A request replayed to any instance must behave identically.
- **Cacheable**: `ETag` + `Cache-Control` let proxies and browsers skip the server entirely. A `304 Not Modified` is the single cheapest response on the internet.
- **Uniform interface**: `GET /orders/{id}` not `/getOrder?id=42`; standard status codes (`201`, `204`, `404`, `409`); `_links` let clients follow relationships without hard-coding URLs (HATEOAS).
- **Layered system**: clients cannot tell whether the response came from a CDN, an API gateway, the Spring app, or a cache — each layer is transparent.
- If the API does not satisfy these, it is **RPC over HTTP**, not REST — which is fine, but call it what it is.

The biggest practical problem with status codes isn't choosing them — it's keeping them consistent as the codebase grows. Without a centralized strategy, different developers return different codes for the same situation. One controller throws `ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)`, another returns `ResponseEntity.status(404)`, and a third returns null and accidentally produces 200. Clients can't predict what to expect, and monitoring dashboards produce misleading alerts.

**The fix is a domain exception hierarchy mapped to status codes in a single `@RestControllerAdvice`.** Each business exception carries its implicit status — `ResourceNotFoundException` is always 404, `ConflictException` is always 409, `ValidationException` is always 422. Controllers throw them freely; the advice class maps them consistently:

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        // 422 for semantic failures, not 400 — syntax is valid, content is wrong
        ProblemDetail detail = ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY);
        detail.setProperty("errors", ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()
            .stream().map(e -> e.getField() + ": " + e.getDefaultMessage()).toList());
        return detail;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ProblemDetail handleUnexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("Unhandled exception", ex); // log full trace, never expose it
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
            "An unexpected error occurred");
    }
}
```

Using Spring 6's `ProblemDetail` returns a standardized RFC 7807 body — `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` — that client teams can depend on across every endpoint. The catch-all at the bottom always returns 500 and **never includes the exception message or stack trace** in the response. Leaking internal exception details in a 500 is one of the most common API security mistakes — attackers use error messages to probe internal structure. Log everything server-side; return nothing client-side.

// Client sends a unique key per logical attempt; retries reuse the same key.
// curl example:
//   curl -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/charges \
//     -H 'Idempotency-Key: 7a9c3f12-b4e8-4e0a-9f77-0b3a8c2d1e55' \
//     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
//     -d '{"amount": 5000, "currency": "USD"}'

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/v1/charges")
class ChargeController {

    private final ChargeService service;
    private final IdempotencyStore store;   // Redis or Postgres

    @PostMapping
    ResponseEntity<ChargeDto> create(
            @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") @NotBlank String idemKey,
            @RequestBody @Valid CreateChargeRequest req) {

        // 1) Look up the key; hash the request body to detect reuse-with-different-body
        String bodyHash = sha256(req);
        Optional<IdempotentRecord> existing = store.find(idemKey);

        if (existing.isPresent()) {
            IdempotentRecord rec = existing.get();
            // Same key + same body -> replay the stored response exactly
            if (rec.requestHash().equals(bodyHash)) {
                return ResponseEntity.status(rec.status())
                    .header("Idempotent-Replayed", "true")
                    .body(rec.response(ChargeDto.class));
            }
            // Same key + different body -> 409; this is a client bug we want to surface
            throw new IdempotencyMismatchException(idemKey);
        }

        // 2) First request for this key — reserve a slot atomically (ms TTL until response known)
        if (!store.tryReserve(idemKey, bodyHash, Duration.ofSeconds(30))) {
            // Another in-flight request has the same key; 409 while it resolves
            throw new IdempotencyInFlightException(idemKey);
        }

        // 3) Execute the real work
        try {
            ChargeDto result = service.createCharge(req);
            store.commit(idemKey, bodyHash, 201, result, Duration.ofHours(24));
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED)
                .header(HttpHeaders.LOCATION, "/v1/charges/" + result.id())
                .body(result);
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            store.fail(idemKey, ex);      // release the reservation so retries can succeed
            throw ex;
        }
    }
}

// Redis-backed store sketch (atomic reservation via SET NX)
interface IdempotencyStore {
    Optional<IdempotentRecord> find(String key);
    boolean tryReserve(String key, String hash, Duration ttl);
    void commit(String key, String hash, int status, Object body, Duration ttl);
    void fail(String key, Throwable cause);
}

record IdempotentRecord(String requestHash, int status, String bodyJson, Instant at) {
    <T> T response(Class<T> type) {
        return JSON.readValue(bodyJson, type);
    }
}

# Resource as the server currently holds it:
#   GET /v1/users/42
#   { "id": 42, "name": "Ada", "email": "ada@acme.com", "role": "admin" }

# --- PUT: full replacement (RFC 7231) -----------------------------------------
# You send the complete desired state. Fields you omit get reset to defaults/null.
curl -X PUT https://api.example.com/v1/users/42 \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
        "id": 42,
        "name": "Ada Lovelace",
        "email": "ada@acme.com",
        "role": "admin"
      }'
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK (or 204 No Content)

# --- PATCH with JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396) -----------------------------------
# Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json
# Only fields present in the patch are updated. `null` clears a field.
curl -X PATCH https://api.example.com/v1/users/42 \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json' \
  -d '{
        "name": "Ada Lovelace",
        "role": null
      }'
# After: { id:42, name:"Ada Lovelace", email:"ada@acme.com", role:null }

# --- PATCH with JSON Patch (RFC 6902) -----------------------------------------
# Content-Type: application/json-patch+json
# An *array of operations*: add/remove/replace/move/copy/test — atomic if the server
# applies them all-or-nothing.
curl -X PATCH https://api.example.com/v1/users/42 \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json-patch+json' \
  -d '[
        { "op": "test",    "path": "/email", "value": "ada@acme.com" },
        { "op": "replace", "path": "/name",  "value": "Ada Lovelace" },
        { "op": "remove",  "path": "/role" },
        { "op": "add",     "path": "/tags/-", "value": "founder" }
      ]'

# The `test` op above gives optimistic concurrency — the patch rejects if
# the email has since changed.

# Spring Boot implementation sketches ------------------------------------------
@PutMapping("/v1/users/{id}")
UserDto replace(@PathVariable long id, @RequestBody @Valid UserDto full) {
    // Idempotent: sending the same body twice yields the same state.
    return userService.replace(id, full);
}

@PatchMapping(value="/v1/users/{id}",
              consumes="application/merge-patch+json")
UserDto mergePatch(@PathVariable long id, @RequestBody JsonNode patch) {
    UserDto cur = userService.get(id);
    JsonNode merged = JsonMergePatch.fromJson(patch).apply(mapper.valueToTree(cur));
    return userService.replace(id, mapper.treeToValue(merged, UserDto.class));
}

@PatchMapping(value="/v1/users/{id}",
              consumes="application/json-patch+json")
UserDto jsonPatch(@PathVariable long id, @RequestBody JsonPatch patch) {
    UserDto cur = userService.get(id);
    JsonNode target = patch.apply(mapper.valueToTree(cur));
    return userService.replace(id, mapper.treeToValue(target, UserDto.class));
}

# Quick rule of thumb:
#  - Client ships full state every time  -> PUT (idempotent by construction)
#  - Client describes a diff             -> PATCH (merge-patch for simple, json-patch for atomic ops)
